    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Replaying the <em>same</em> batch twice in order is harmless everywhere, because every row in it has the same
      position the second time. The danger is a replay that puts older content after newer content.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-not-timestamp" aria-labelledby="why-not-timestamp-title">
    <h2 id="why-not-timestamp-title">Why the vendors&rsquo; own examples are not the whole answer</h2>
    <p>
      Several vendor pages show a timestamp as the ordering input: BigQuery&rsquo;s single-section example is a
      processing timestamp, Hudi&rsquo;s SQL guide suggests a field &ldquo;that denotes the time of the event&rdquo;,
      Delta&rsquo;s change-data example orders by a <code>time</code> column and the Debezium Server page lists
      <code>__source_ts_ns</code> for its Iceberg sink. Databricks&rsquo; multi-column example uses a
      timestamp and an ID to break ties. These examples are fine as illustrations of the setting, but a timestamp can tie
      for one key and can be skewed between hosts, so do not use one as the ordering key when the source gives you a log
      position. The log position is the safer key: it says which change committed later, and a replay carries the
      position it had the first time. Why, and how to compare positions: see
      <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making sinks bulletproof</a>. If your source has no total order per
      key (some <a href="{{ '/non-relational/' | url }}">non-relational sources</a>), say so in the design and pick the
      best monotonic field it does give you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="targets" aria-labelledby="targets-title">
    <h2 id="targets-title">Target by target</h2>
    <p>
      The snippets assume a staging table with <code>id</code>, <code>op</code>, a numeric <code>source_lsn</code> and an
      ordinal <code>source_ordinal</code> that separates changes sharing a position. All are marked untested.
    </p>

    <h3 id="bigquery">BigQuery CDC ingestion</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Default.</strong> You stream rows through the Storage Write API default stream with
      <code>_CHANGE_TYPE</code> set to <code>UPSERT</code> or <code>DELETE</code>. With identical primary keys the record
      most recently ingested by BigQuery wins. The default stream is documented as at-least-once, so duplicates are
      expected.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Failure with arrival order.</strong> A replayed old <code>UPSERT</code> is ingested later than the new one
      and overwrites it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe configuration.</strong> Declare the primary key, then always set
      <code>_CHANGE_SEQUENCE_NUMBER</code>. It is a <code>STRING</code> of up to four hexadecimal sections separated by
      <code>/</code>, up to 16 hex characters each, compared section by section as unsigned numbers; the larger wins, and
      equal values fall back to ingestion time. Mixing rows with and without it gives unpredictable ordering. Put the log
      position first and the ordinal second, for example <code>&lt;lsn hex&gt;/&lt;ordinal hex&gt;</code>. A Postgres LSN
      printed as <code>16/B374D848</code> is already in that shape; that it compares in LSN order is my reading of the two
      pages, so test it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Deletes.</strong> <code>DELETE</code> removes the row; BigQuery keeps delete operations for a two-day window
      so that out-of-order ones apply. A delete counts as applied only once
      <code>upsert_stream_apply_watermark</code> passes it. The page does not say what happens to an older
      <code>UPSERT</code> that arrives after that window: check. If you need the delete to be permanent, send an
      <code>UPSERT</code> with a <code>deleted</code> flag and the delete&rsquo;s sequence number instead (a design choice,
      not a vendor feature). Tables with active CDC do not allow DML <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> or
      <code>MERGE</code>, and key uniqueness is not enforced.
    </p>
    <details open>
      <summary>BigQuery: table and sequence string (untested)</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Untested: not run against a live BigQuery project.
CREATE TABLE analytics.customers (
  id INT64 PRIMARY KEY NOT ENFORCED,
  email STRING,
  deleted BOOL
)
CLUSTER BY id
OPTIONS (max_staleness = INTERVAL 10 MINUTE);</code></pre>
      <pre><code class="language-python"># Untested. Build the pseudocolumn values for each Storage Write API row.
def change_sequence_number(source_lsn: int, ordinal: int) -&gt; str:
    # Two hex sections: the log position first, then the ordinal.
    return f"{source_lsn:X}/{ordinal:X}"

row = {
    "id": 7,
    "email": "a@example.com",
    "_CHANGE_TYPE": "UPSERT",  # "DELETE" for op = 'd'
    "_CHANGE_SEQUENCE_NUMBER": change_sequence_number(200, 0),
}</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h3 id="databricks">Databricks AUTO CDC</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Default.</strong> There is none: <code>KEYS</code> and <code>SEQUENCE BY</code> are required, and the
      pipeline orders events by the sequencing column. The documentation asks for a column that is a monotonically
      increasing representation of the correct event order, with one distinct update per key at each value, and does not
      support <code>NULL</code> sequencing values. Its own sample drops an update at sequence 5 that arrives after one at 6.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Failure with a timestamp.</strong> Two changes to a key with the same timestamp break the &ldquo;one distinct
      update per key at each value&rdquo; requirement; what happens then is not documented: check. A skewed timestamp
      orders changes wrongly without any error.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe configuration.</strong> Sequence by the position, with the ordinal as the second field of a
      <code>STRUCT</code> (ordering is by the first field, then the second).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Deletes.</strong> <code>APPLY AS DELETE WHEN</code> treats an event as a delete. To handle out-of-order data
      the deleted row is retained as a tombstone for two days by default, configurable with
      <code>pipelines.cdc.tombstoneGCThresholdInSeconds</code>; the documentation says to set it above the longest delay
      between event arrival and pipeline execution. The SQL reference words this for SCD type 2 targets, so confirm it
      for SCD type 1 on your runtime. A replay older than the tombstone retention is not covered.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Databricks: AUTO CDC flow (untested)</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Untested: not run in a Lakeflow pipeline.
CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE customers_current;

CREATE FLOW customers_apply AS AUTO CDC INTO customers_current
FROM stream(cdc.customers_changes)
KEYS (id)
APPLY AS DELETE WHEN op = 'd'
SEQUENCE BY STRUCT(source_lsn, source_ordinal)
COLUMNS * EXCEPT (op, source_lsn, source_ordinal)
STORED AS SCD TYPE 1;</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h3 id="hudi">Apache Hudi</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Default.</strong> The merge mode is inferred. With no ordering field it is
      <code>COMMIT_TIME_ORDERING</code>: the record from the latest write wins, and the documentation says this expects
      input in strict order, with a database change log ordered by LSN as its example. With one or more ordering fields
      it is <code>EVENT_TIME_ORDERING</code>: the larger value wins, regardless of commit time. The table-level setting is
      <code>hoodie.table.ordering.fields</code> (the older <code>precombine</code> names are deprecated on the pages
      read; Flink uses <code>ordering.fields</code>). Do not change the merge mode after the table exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Failure with arrival order.</strong> A late old change overwrites a newer one under
      <code>COMMIT_TIME_ORDERING</code>. With an event-time field, a field that is a timestamp has the problems above.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe configuration.</strong> Make the ordering field the log position. Hudi Streamer&rsquo;s Debezium guide
      already does this: <code>--source-ordering-field _event_lsn</code> for PostgreSQL, and a field derived from the
      binlog file and position for MySQL. Multiple ordering fields compare first to second, so list the position first and
      the ordinal second. The Hudi source (<code>BufferedRecordMergerFactory</code> on the master branch, which may differ
      from your release) shows why: <code>shouldKeepNewerRecord</code> keeps the newer record when ordering values are
      equal (<code>compareTo(...) &gt;= 0</code>). With the LSN alone as ordering field, two changes that share an LSN
      therefore resolve by arrival order. The guide describes the payload comparison as applying when
      deduplicating within a batch, and the payload classes it uses are marked deprecated on the merger page, so check
      your version.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Deletes.</strong> A delete operation, or <code>_hoodie_is_deleted = true</code>, removes the record, and the
      Debezium payload applies <code>d</code> events as deletes. In the same source,
      <code>deltaMergeDeleteRecord</code> discards a delete whose ordering value is lower than the stored row&rsquo;s as
      obsolete. A removed key leaves no marker, so my reasoning from that code is that a late, lower-ordered upsert can
      insert it again; the documentation pages do not say, so check your version, or keep your own
      <code>deleted</code> flag and position in the row.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Hudi: table with an ordering field (untested)</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Untested: not run on Spark with Hudi.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customers_hudi (
  id BIGINT,
  email STRING,
  deleted BOOLEAN,
  source_lsn BIGINT,
  source_ordinal BIGINT
) USING hudi
TBLPROPERTIES (
  type = 'mor',
  primaryKey = 'id',
  orderingFields = 'source_lsn,source_ordinal',
  recordMergeMode = 'EVENT_TIME_ORDERING'
);</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h3 id="iceberg">Apache Iceberg</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Default.</strong> An Iceberg table has no ordering column. Flink&rsquo;s upsert mode (v2 table format, a
      primary key, <code>write.upsert.enabled</code> or <code>upsert-enabled</code>) writes row-level deletes by equality
      fields plus the new row. Under the spec, an equality delete applies only to data files with a strictly lower data
      sequence number, and sequence numbers are assigned per commit. My reading: the later commit wins, whatever position
      the data carried. The upsert section documents no version column.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Failure.</strong> A replay that commits after the newer change replaces it. In Spark, <code>MERGE INTO</code>
      errors if more than one source row can update a target row, so deduplicate the source first.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe configuration.</strong> Order before the sink, or merge with a guard on the position column and the
      ordinal, keyed on the primary key (snippet below). That guarded <code>MERGE</code> is the safe configuration.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Debezium Server Iceberg sink is not a substitute.</strong> Its
      <code>debezium.sink.iceberg.upsert-dedup-column</code> has no default: it is optional in the sink&rsquo;s
      <code>IcebergConfig.java</code>, and the documentation table on debezium.io, which lists
      <code>__source_ts_ns</code>, disagrees with the code and with the project&rsquo;s own docs. In
      <code>IcebergTableOperator.addToTable</code>, deduplication runs per batch only, when upsert is on and the table has
      identifier fields. With the column blank, the last event of the batch for a key wins. With it set, the
      value is compared as a <code>Long</code> (null sorts lowest), and equal values fall back to operation priority
      <code>d</code> over <code>u</code> over <code>r</code> over <code>c</code>. Nothing in that code compares an event with
      the stored row, so an older value in a later batch overwrites a newer one: it gives no cross-batch protection. To put
      the position in the record, use the <code>add.fields</code> option of the event-flattening transform (<code>lsn</code>
      becomes <code>__lsn</code>), and set <code>delete.tombstone.handling.mode=rewrite</code>: without it a delete event
      lacks the added fields, so it has no <code>__lsn</code> and loses to any update in the batch. This is my reading of
      the repository code at master (<a href="https://github.com/memiiso/debezium-server-iceberg" rel="noopener">memiiso/debezium-server-iceberg</a>);
      check your version.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Deletes.</strong> Equality deletes are a v2/v3 feature; the draft v4 spec, which is not yet formally
      adopted, prohibits writing new ones, so check your format version and engine. The Debezium sink keeps deletes as
      <code>__deleted = true</code> rows by default (<code>upsert-keep-deletes</code>), which is the marker pattern.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Iceberg: guarded MERGE INTO with a marker (untested)</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Untested: not run on Spark with Iceberg.
-- stg is already one row per id (latest by source_lsn, source_ordinal).
MERGE INTO prod.db.customers t
USING stg s
ON t.id = s.id
WHEN MATCHED AND (t.source_lsn &lt; s.source_lsn
     OR (t.source_lsn = s.source_lsn AND t.source_ordinal &lt; s.source_ordinal)) THEN
  UPDATE SET t.email = s.email, t.deleted = (s.op = 'd'),
             t.source_lsn = s.source_lsn, t.source_ordinal = s.source_ordinal
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  INSERT (id, email, deleted, source_lsn, source_ordinal)
  VALUES (s.id, s.email, (s.op = 'd'), s.source_lsn, s.source_ordinal);</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h3 id="delta">Delta Lake: MERGE and change data feed</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Default.</strong> Delta has no ordering setting; <code>MERGE</code> applies the source rows you give it. A
      merge can fail when several source rows match one target row, and the documentation tells you to reduce the source to
      the latest change per key first. Its change-data example does that with a <code>time</code> column
      (&ldquo;can be replaced by other ordering id&rdquo;), then applies deletes with
      <code>whenMatchedDelete</code> and inserts only rows that are not deleted. Nothing compares against the stored row.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Failure.</strong> With that example, a replayed older batch overwrites the newer row, and an older update
      after a delete finds no match and is inserted: the row is back.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe configuration.</strong> Dedupe the source by position, add a guard on the stored position, and keep the
      delete as a marker. In <code>foreachBatch</code> the documentation says the merge must be idempotent, because a restart
      can apply the same batch again.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Change data feed.</strong> The feed records row-level changes <em>of a Delta table</em>, with
      <code>_change_type</code>, <code>_commit_version</code> and <code>_commit_timestamp</code>. It is off by default, only
      captures changes after you enable it, and its files follow table retention, so <code>VACUUM</code> removes them. Its
      version is the Delta table&rsquo;s own commit version, not a source position: a source of changes for tables
      downstream of this one, not a substitute for the source log.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Delta: guarded MERGE with a marker (untested)</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Untested: not run on Delta Lake. stg is one row per id.
MERGE INTO customers t
USING stg s
ON t.id = s.id
WHEN MATCHED AND (t.source_lsn &lt; s.source_lsn
     OR (t.source_lsn = s.source_lsn AND t.source_ordinal &lt; s.source_ordinal)) THEN
  UPDATE SET t.email = s.email, t.deleted = (s.op = 'd'),
             t.source_lsn = s.source_lsn, t.source_ordinal = s.source_ordinal
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  INSERT (id, email, deleted, source_lsn, source_ordinal)
  VALUES (s.id, s.email, (s.op = 'd'), s.source_lsn, s.source_ordinal);</code></pre>
    </details>

    <h3 id="snowflake">Snowflake: MERGE and streams</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Default.</strong> <code>MERGE</code> has no ordering. If one target row matches several source rows for an
      <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code>, the result is nondeterministic: with
      <code>ERROR_ON_NONDETERMINISTIC_MERGE</code> at its default of TRUE the merge errors; with FALSE one row is chosen
      and which one is not defined. A stream is a bookmark (an offset) on a table&rsquo;s transactional versions that
      returns the changes since then, with <code>METADATA$ACTION</code>, <code>METADATA$ISUPDATE</code> and
      <code>METADATA$ROW_ID</code>. Put one over the table your loader lands CDC rows in and it orders by the landing
      table&rsquo;s versions, not by the source log. Querying a stream does not advance it; consuming it in a DML
      statement does.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Failure.</strong> A stream over the landing table faithfully hands you the replayed duplicates and the
      swapped pair. An unguarded <code>MERGE</code> from it resurrects stale rows exactly as in the table above. The
      transactional stream consumption makes the landing-to-target hop safe to retry; it does nothing for the hop before it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Safe configuration.</strong> Land the position as a column, read an append-only stream or the landing table,
      reduce to one row per key with <code>QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER()</code> by position, and merge with the guard and marker. The
      <a href="{{ '/cloud-labs/snowflake-cdc/' | url }}">Snowflake lab</a> shows the loading side.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Snowflake: guarded MERGE from a stream (untested)</summary>
      <pre><code class="language-sql">-- Untested: not run on Snowflake. cdc_stream is an append-only stream
-- on the landing table cdc_landing(id, email, op, source_lsn, source_ordinal).
MERGE INTO customers t
USING (
  SELECT id, email, op, source_lsn, source_ordinal
  FROM cdc_stream
  QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
    PARTITION BY id ORDER BY source_lsn DESC, source_ordinal DESC) = 1
) s
ON t.id = s.id
WHEN MATCHED AND (t.source_lsn &lt; s.source_lsn
     OR (t.source_lsn = s.source_lsn AND t.source_ordinal &lt; s.source_ordinal)) THEN
  UPDATE SET email = s.email, deleted = (s.op = 'd'),
             source_lsn = s.source_lsn, source_ordinal = s.source_ordinal
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  INSERT (id, email, deleted, source_lsn, source_ordinal)
  VALUES (s.id, s.email, (s.op = 'd'), s.source_lsn, s.source_ordinal);</code></pre>
    </details>
    <p class="muted">
      A <code>NULL</code> stored position makes the guard <code>NULL</code> and the row is never updated: backfill it, or
      compare <code>COALESCE(t.source_lsn, -1)</code>. Positions are comparable only within one source log lineage
      (see the lineage note in <a href="{{ '/partitioning/' | url }}#recon">Making sinks bulletproof</a>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="deletes" aria-labelledby="deletes-title">
    <h2 id="deletes-title">Delete markers, not physical deletes or tombstones alone</h2>
    <p>
      A delete in the log is a normal change event (<code>op = 'd'</code>). The Kafka
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/' | url }}#tombstone">tombstone</a> that may follow is a compaction hint for the topic; sinks
      should act on the event, not wait for the tombstone. Whatever the target, a physical delete takes the version with
      it, so a replayed older update finds nothing to lose to. Either keep a marker row (a
      <code>deleted</code> flag plus the delete&rsquo;s position) or rely on a target feature that keeps the delete for a
      stated time, and know the time: two days in BigQuery and Databricks by default. When the marker may finally go is
      the subject of a later module on deletes that stay deleted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="eos" aria-labelledby="eos-title">
    <h2 id="eos-title">Where exactly-once stops</h2>
    <p>
      Say it per hop. <strong>Database to Kafka:</strong> Debezium provides at-least-once delivery and has no
      deduplication layer of its own. Its documentation (from 3.3) describes an opt-in exactly-once mode for source
      connectors on Kafka Connect, built on KIP-618: Kafka Connect 3.3.0 or later in distributed mode,
      <code>exactly.once.source.support=enabled</code> on the workers and <code>exactly.once.support=required</code> on the
      connector. It covers that one hop, and the same page says it is unclear whether the underlying transaction
      implementation is fully correct, citing open Kafka issues. <strong>Kafka to your target:</strong> a separate system
      with no shared transaction. BigQuery CDC ingestion needs the default stream, which Google documents as at-least-once.
      The correctness you can claim at the target is exactly-once <em>processing</em>: duplicates and reordering
      happen, and the position-ordered idempotent write makes them harmless. See
      <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/' | url }}#idempotency">Exactly-once semantics</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try-it" aria-labelledby="try-it-title">
    <h2 id="try-it-title">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Open the <a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}">Change Feed Playground</a> and pick the
      <strong>Re-insert after Update</strong> scenario (its id is <code>snapshot-replay</code>). In its event list the ledger row
      <code>LED-100</code> is updated to version <code>v2</code>, then written again with its old <code>v1</code> values, then
      <code>v3</code> follows. That re-insert is an ordinary later source write, not a redelivered old event, so every target
      applies it, and a position guard would too. Write down what each target above would hold after the <code>v1</code> write,
      then work out what would change if the same <code>v1</code> record arrived a second time after <code>v3</code>: that is
      the case your default and the position-based setting disagree on. The Re-insert scenario cannot produce it, but two
      labs can, for one log lane and one key:
      <a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}?try=replay-guard#simulator">Replay against a guarded sink</a> delivers old changes
      again after newer ones, and
      <a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}?try=ts-vs-position#simulator">ts_ms against log position</a> shows a timestamp
      guard keeping the wrong change. They open the playground, a separate tool that contacts a third-party database
      (see the <a href="{{ '/privacy/' | url }}#playground">privacy note</a>). The playground compares capture methods and
      models a guard in general terms; it does not simulate these six targets, so mapping what you see onto each target is
      on paper.
    </p>
  </section>

